l we've had a  little bit of a change since  then because now you have the american  academy of pediatrics and the children's  hospital association if i can put this  on the screen  between july 9th and august 6th there  were 179  plus thousand cases of children getting  the coronavirus that's an increase of  90 percent over four weeks does that  number give you pause at all secretary  well we know that children are going to  get the virus  adults are going to get the virus but  the the facts have been  uh that the virus has not impacted  children  in the same way that even the average  flu season does  the number of of children that have  actually succumbed to the virus versus  the flu  is like one-sixth the amount of an  annual rate  and so again the focus really needs to  be  on doing the right things you know the  cdc has provided  a lot of guidance and a lot of  recommendations  many schools have reopened and they're  doing so successfully when they follow  this guidance  and they take the matter seriously but  the point is  they're doing the things some of them  hard some of them  unusual for you know a normal school  circumstance  but they're doing the things that need  to be done to get  kids back into the classroom and back  into their learning  yeah the president floating this idea  secretary devos of  having the money follow the children and  the schools that open giving them some  money and the schools that don't pulling  back some money and giving it to the  kids  the pushback has been pretty severe from  democrats and  educators across the country your  thoughts on that  well we make a promise when we invest in  education and that is to actually  educate  children and that promise is made to  their families and if they're  if that is not going to happen the  president's absolutely  absolutely right parents should have the  power  to take those resources and find a  school or an environment that's going to  work for them  we're seeing a lot of schools a